A list runs on the device. The SDK matches the conditions against the local index of the decrypted content, so a list works offline. Each `list()` takes the same options: `where` to filter, `orderBy` to sort, and `take` to cap the number of results.

## Listing

`list()` does not return an array. It returns a list that you iterate with `for await`, or collect with `.all()`. The list loads the items in batches as the loop reaches them.

```ts
for await (const event of events.list({ where: { calendar: work.id } })) {
  console.log(event.title)
}

const all = await events.list({ where: { calendar: work.id } }).all()
```

A list is not a promise, so `await events.list()` does not compile. Call `.all()` or loop over the list.

Stopping a `for await` loop stops loading further batches.

`containers.list()` works the same way for containers, and `notifications.list()` for the REST resources.

## Filtering

A `where` object names fields of the item, and the container under the key of the container model. A plain value matches equal values, and an operator object compares the field in a richer way.

```ts
const weekEvents = await events
  .list({
    where: {
      calendar: work.id,
      start: { gte: new Date('2026-10-12T00:00:00Z'), lt: new Date('2026-10-19T00:00:00Z') },
    },
  })
  .all()
```

Each field type takes its own operators:

- Every field takes `equals`, `not`, `in` and `notIn`, and `null` matches a missing value.
- Numbers and dates take `lt`, `lte`, `gt` and `gte` as well.
- Text takes `contains`, `startsWith` and `endsWith`. Add `mode: 'insensitive'` to ignore case.

`AND`, `OR` and `NOT` combine conditions. Each takes one condition or an array of them:

```ts
const matching = await events
  .list({
    where: {
      OR: [
        { title: { contains: 'standup', mode: 'insensitive' } },
        { description: { contains: 'standup', mode: 'insensitive' } },
      ],
      deleted: false,
    },
  })
  .all()
```

The container can be filtered by its own fields with `is`. This finds the events of every calendar with the color blue:

```ts
const blueEvents = await events
  .list({ where: { calendar: { is: { color: CategoryColor.blue } } } })
  .all()
```

The compiler rejects a field the schema does not have, and an operator that does not fit the type of a field. A query that the device still refuses throws an `InvalidQueryError`.

## Ordering

`orderBy` sorts by one or more fields of the schema, each `asc` or `desc`. Give an array to sort by several fields in turn:

```ts
const sorted = await events
  .list({
    where: { calendar: work.id },
    orderBy: [{ start: 'asc' }, { title: 'asc' }],
  })
  .all()
```

To sort by when a node was created or last changed, order by `_meta`. A `_meta` order must stand alone, with exactly one of `created` or `updated`:

```ts
const newest = await events.list({ orderBy: { _meta: { created: 'desc' } }, take: 10 }).all()
```

## Limiting, paging and counting

<MarginNote>Paging is handled by the list. The SDK does not expose cursors.</MarginNote>

`take` caps the number of results. With a `_meta` order, the sort runs first and `take` keeps the first results of it.

`count` returns how many nodes match a `where`, from the index, without decrypting the content:

```ts
const total = await events.count({ where: { calendar: work.id } })
```

<CodeAside {...searchExample}>

## Search

Items have a `search` method that matches the fields the schema marks as searchable, case-insensitively. In the calendar schema these are the title, the description and the keywords of an event.

</CodeAside>

## What a list leaves out

A list skips shortcuts and deleted nodes. It also skips an item whose content cannot be read, and it reports no error for it. `get(id)` throws for the same item, so use it when a failure must be seen.

A container that cannot be read is still listed, with only its `id` and `_meta`, so it still shows up in the list.
